+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `geo`
+
+Thirteen hegel property tests were added across seven existing test files (plus one
+shared generator in `geo/src/utils.rs`). Ten pass; three are deliberately left
+failing as canonical witnesses of three distinct suspected library bugs.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Shared generator: `star_polygon` (`geo/src/utils.rs`, `pbt_gens` module) builds
+simple star-shaped polygons — vertices at strictly increasing angles around a
+centre, every normalised angular gap < π, so the ring is provably
+non-self-intersecting. Centre ±1e3, radii 0.5..=1e3, 3–24 vertices (bounds keep
+areas above f64 noise; documented in the generator as protecting test
+tolerances, not library contracts).
+
+| # | Test (file) | Property | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `pbt_union_with_self_preserves_area` (`bool_ops/tests.rs`) | area(A ∪ A) ≈ area(A) | `BooleanOps` docs: set operations, well-defined on valid geometries |
+| 2 | `pbt_union_intersection_inclusion_exclusion` (`bool_ops/tests.rs`) | area(A∪B) + area(A∩B) ≈ area(A) + area(B) | same; inclusion–exclusion is a theorem for set ops |
+| 3 | `pbt_difference_and_intersection_partition_union` (`bool_ops/tests.rs`) | area(A∩B) + area(A\B) + area(B\A) ≈ area(A∪B) | same; all four terms from op outputs so snapping cancels |
+| 4 | `pbt_convex_hull_contains_all_input_points` (`convex_hull/test.rs`) | every input point on/left of every directed hull edge | trait docs: "Returns the convex hull … always oriented counter-clockwise"; oracle = exact `robust::orient2d` |
+| 5 | `pbt_convex_hull_is_convex_and_ccw` (`convex_hull/test.rs`) | no clockwise turn between consecutive hull vertices | same docs; restricted to exact i8 grid while bug #1 is open |
+| 6 | `pbt_convex_hull_no_panic_on_any_finite_coords` (`convex_hull/test.rs`) | hull never panics on finite coords; ring closed | no-crash robustness (skill Tier-1); finite coords are valid per `Validation` |
+| 7 | `pbt_centroid_translation_equivariant` (`centroid.rs`) | centroid(translate(P)) ≈ centroid(P) + (dx,dy) | implementation shifts by first ring coord for exactly this; existing unit test `centroid_polygon_numerical_stability` |
+| 8 | `pbt_signed_area_translation_invariant` (`area.rs`) | signed_area invariant under translation | `sum_line_determinants` doc comment (shift trick); existing `area_polygon_numerical_stability` test |
+| 9 | `pbt_signed_area_negates_when_ring_reversed` (`area.rs`) | area(reverse(ring)) ≈ −area(ring) | `Area` trait doc example shows 30 → −30 on reverse |
+| 10 | `pbt_contains_implies_intersects` (`contains/mod.rs`) | contains(P, pt) ⇒ intersects(P, pt) | `Contains` docs (DE-9IM interior semantics) vs `Intersects` (any shared point) |
+| 11 | `pbt_simplify_matches_simplify_idx` (`simplify.rs`) | `simplify_idx` indices strictly increasing, in bounds, and map exactly onto `simplify` output; epsilon fully unconstrained (incl. NaN/±inf) | `simplify_idx` docs: "the indices of the points retained by `Simplify::simplify`" |
+| 12 | `pbt_simplify_never_increases_length` (`simplify.rs`) | Euclidean length never increases under simplification | RDP retains a vertex subsequence (triangle inequality); same property as the repo's `fuzz/fuzz_targets/simplify.rs` |
+| 13 | `pbt_star_polygons_are_valid` (`validation/tests.rs`) | the star-polygon construction is accepted by `Validation` | construction proof (angular monotonicity ⇒ simple); guards the generator all other properties rely on |
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test -p geo`: **1165 passed, 3 failed, 2 ignored** — the only failures
+  are the three canonical suspected-bug witnesses (#4, #6, #11 above), left
+  failing per instructions. No pre-existing tests broke.
+- Liveness check performed: temporarily inverted the assertion in
+  `pbt_contains_implies_intersects`; hegel shrank to a minimal unit-triangle +
+  point-(0,0) counterexample. Reverted, `.hegel/` database removed and
+  gitignored.
+- Exploratory 10× run (`test_cases = 1000` on all tests, then reverted):
+  found one failure invisible at the default count —
+  `pbt_convex_hull_is_convex_and_ccw` failed on unrestricted floats via the
+  same pivot-selection bug as #4 (clockwise turn at (0,0) among points
+  including (2.5e16, −1.7e149)). Per the skill, that sibling was then
+  restricted to the exact integer grid with a comment referencing the
+  canonical test. All other tests also passed at 10×.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `ConvexHull` returns non-convex / self-intersecting "hulls" (wrong results)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel):
+  `MultiPoint [(0,0), (-1, 9150170671525436), (63,0), (0,1)]` — the returned
+  ring contains directed edge `(0,0) → (0,1)` with input point `(63,0)`
+  strictly to its right (verified with exact `robust::orient2d`). A related
+  input `[(0,1), (0,0), (0,2), (-1.23e16, 1.23e16), (1,0)]` returns
+  `[(0,0), (1,0), (0,2), (0,1), (0,2), (-1.23e16, 1.23e16), (0,0)]` — vertex
+  `(0,2)` appears twice; the ring is self-intersecting.
+- **Why it's a library bug**: the docs promise "the convex hull"; the output is
+  not convex and can even be an invalid polygon. Coordinates ~1e15–1e16 are
+  unremarkable (e.g. metres in a projected CRS would not get there, but
+  timestamps/scaled data do), and geo already pays for robust orientation
+  predicates elsewhere in the same algorithm.
+- **Code path**: `geo/src/algorithm/convex_hull/qhull.rs`, `hull_set` — the
+  farthest-point pivot is selected with a naive dot product
+  (`p_orth.x * p_diff.x + p_orth.y * p_diff.y`). When candidate scores are
+  ~1e32 and differ by ~1, they collide after rounding and an interior point can
+  be chosen as pivot; `is_ccw` (robust) and the pivot selection (non-robust)
+  then disagree.
+- **Witness test**: `pbt_convex_hull_contains_all_input_points` (kept failing).
+
+### 2. `ConvexHull` panics on finite coordinates (release builds included)
+
+- **Minimal counterexample**:
+  `MultiPoint [(0,0), (0,0), (1,0), (-7.076196536963262e206, -8.648940011347372e215)]`
+  → `panicked at qhull.rs:156:51: called Option::unwrap() on a None value`.
+- **Why it's a library bug**: all coordinates are finite f64 (valid per
+  `Validation`); a total function returning a hull should not panic.
+- **Code path**: same dot product in `hull_set` overflows to ±inf, `inf − inf`
+  gives NaN, and `.max_by(|a, b| a.partial_cmp(b).unwrap())` unwraps `None`.
+- **Witness test**: `pbt_convex_hull_no_panic_on_any_finite_coords` (kept
+  failing).
+
+### 3. `Simplify` drops points farther than epsilon; debug builds panic
+
+- **Minimal counterexample**:
+  `LINESTRING(0 -8.988465674311469e307, 0 0, 3 -0)`, `epsilon = 2.0`.
+  - Debug: `panicked at simplify.rs:108:5` — the library's own
+    `debug_assert_ne!(farthest_index, 0)`.
+  - Release (verified): returns `LINESTRING(0 -8.99e307, 3 -0)`, silently
+    discarding the middle point whose true perpendicular distance to the
+    result is ~3.0 > epsilon — violating the documented contract
+    "points closer than `epsilon` distance from the simplified output may be
+    discarded".
+- **Code path**: `compute_rdp` (`geo/src/algorithm/simplify.rs`) calls
+  `Euclidean.distance(coord, &Line)`; the internal cross product overflows f64
+  to infinity and the distance becomes NaN, so the farthest-point fold never
+  advances past its `(0usize, 0.0)` seed.
+- **Witness test**: `pbt_simplify_matches_simplify_idx` (kept failing; the
+  sibling length test is bounded to ±1e150 with a comment referencing this
+  one).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact (tight-relative-tolerance) area identities for `BooleanOps`.** The
+  first formulation used a 1e-9 relative tolerance and failed on thin
+  triangles (area moved by ~3.4e-7 for extent ~23.5 under union-with-self).
+  Investigation: the i_overlay backend snaps input coordinates onto an integer
+  grid scaled to the combined bounding box (~extent × 2⁻²⁵ per step; the
+  repo's own `test_unary_union_errors` comment confirms the grid design), so
+  exact area preservation was never promised. Replaced with a snapping-aware
+  tolerance `2 · extent · 2⁻²⁵ · total_perimeter · 4` derived from the
+  measured error — the properties still fail loudly for logic bugs, which
+  produce area errors on the scale of whole regions.
+- **Hull correctness on unbounded finite floats.** The original point
+  generator allowed coords up to ±1.8e308; there `robust::orient2d` itself
+  returns NaN (coordinate *differences* overflow before the predicate sees
+  them), so the *oracle* — not the library — failed on a correct degenerate
+  hull. The orientation-checked tests are bounded to ±1e150 (documented as
+  protecting the oracle), and a separate unbounded no-panic test keeps the
+  full range covered (and found bug #2).
+
+Nothing else was weakened: no property assertion was loosened to hide a
+library failure.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well and its advice was directly load-bearing:
+
+- **The mandatory 10× exploratory run paid off concretely**: the convexity
+  property only failed at 1000 cases, never at the default 100. Without step 6
+  I would have shipped it green and unrestricted.
+- **The "canonical failing test + excluded siblings" guidance** mapped cleanly
+  onto the three bugs. One gap: it assumes the buggy input region has a clean
+  boundary. For precision-loss bugs (#1) there is no magnitude threshold below
+  which the naive arithmetic is safe except a *much* smaller exact domain
+  (small integers). A sentence like "for precision bugs, restrict the sibling
+  to an exactly-representable domain rather than trying to bound magnitudes"
+  would have saved deliberation.
+- **Shrinking quality was excellent** — e.g. `[(0,0), (-1, 9150170671525436),
+  (63,0), (0,1)]` is a four-point, mostly-single-digit counterexample for a
+  subtle robustness bug, and the liveness-check counterexample shrank to a
+  unit triangle and the origin.
+- **Rust reference accuracy**: everything I used compiled first try
+  (`#[hegel::composite]` with parameters and `pub(crate)` visibility, boxed
+  generator cloning per gotcha 10, `tuples!`, `min_size(n).max_size(n)`,
+  drawing size separately). The only compile error was geo-specific (E0034
+  method ambiguity because a deprecated `SimplifyIdx` trait duplicates
+  `simplify_idx`); gotcha 12 covers name collisions but could mention
+  *method* ambiguity from deprecated/duplicate traits, fixed by a
+  fully-qualified call.
+- **No global case-count override (gotcha 13)** was the main friction: the 10×
+  pass across 13 tests in 7 files required a sed over `#[hegel::test]`
+  attributes and a revert. An env-var override would make step 6 much cheaper.
+- **Test oracles from sibling dependencies**: using the `robust` crate (already
+  a geo dependency) as an exact oracle was decisive for the hull bugs. The
+  skill's Oracle category could mention that a crate's *own dependencies*
+  often contain an exact/reference primitive usable as an independent oracle.
+- The failure database replay (`.hegel/`) made iterating on known failures
+  instant; the skill's advice to gitignore it up front was correct.
